00:12:28.320 --> 00:12:29.400
whatever scale we need.

00:12:29.400 --> 00:12:30.840
And then they put in the extra effort

00:12:30.840 --> 00:12:34.280
to make sure that that is there
from the beginning in our industry,

00:12:35.400 --> 00:12:37.200
very few technologies

00:12:37.200 --> 00:12:41.840
last for like a decade plus,
and the rest is changing.

00:12:41.840 --> 00:12:43.120
Often.

00:12:43.120 --> 00:12:47.920
And that's why I personally don't invest,
or I wouldn't design a solution

00:12:47.920 --> 00:12:52.320
that would last like ten, 20, 30 years,
unlike what we did in the 80s or 90s.

00:12:52.320 --> 00:12:54.440
That's what
that's how that was the design mentality.

00:12:54.440 --> 00:12:57.440
But things were way
less complicated than they are today,

00:12:58.080 --> 00:13:00.120
and the scale was different.

00:13:00.120 --> 00:13:03.080
So I would always come with the mindset

00:13:03.080 --> 00:13:05.520
that I'm just going to design
for the next order of magnitude,

00:13:05.520 --> 00:13:06.960
and then I'm going to evolve it
from there.

00:13:06.960 --> 00:13:09.960
And that's going to require
another round of investment.

00:13:10.040 --> 00:13:13.320
There's going to be continual investments
in this and that business

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:17.080
people are not going to love this
because that makes accounting much harder.

00:13:17.080 --> 00:13:20.880
That makes strategic finance much harder
because they cannot predict

00:13:20.880 --> 00:13:23.640
or anticipate
the costs that might come in the future.

00:13:23.640 --> 00:13:25.920
They cannot extrapolate from today.

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:28.400
You know,
they don't like this fuzziness software.

00:13:28.400 --> 00:13:29.760
It's all risk. Yes.

00:13:29.760 --> 00:13:32.840
So and also like they cannot calculate
like their bottom line

00:13:32.840 --> 00:13:35.880
or projections for the future,
which are things that are very realistic,

00:13:35.880 --> 00:13:40.000
like publicly, publicly traded companies
want to be able to do these projections,

00:13:40.000 --> 00:13:44.200
and they want to be able to say that, hey,
we're not going to be spending more here

00:13:44.760 --> 00:13:45.960
than on evolving our software,

00:13:45.960 --> 00:13:49.400
but we're going to make much more revenue
and increase our bottom line and margin.

00:13:49.680 --> 00:13:51.480
That's what businesses look for.

00:13:51.480 --> 00:13:52.720
So it's understandable.

00:13:52.720 --> 00:13:56.040
But from a software perspective,
there needs to be regular investments.

00:13:56.040 --> 00:13:57.760
And I think the only approachable way,

00:13:57.760 --> 00:14:00.760
which is something we do at
GitHub, is to continuously revisit

00:14:00.840 --> 00:14:03.360
these investments on a quarterly

00:14:04.360 --> 00:14:07.360
by quarterly, half a year or a year basis.

00:14:07.800 --> 00:14:08.600
Right.

00:14:08.600 --> 00:14:11.600
We can make projections
for the next three, four, five years,

00:14:11.680 --> 00:14:14.600
but the confidence in these projections
is less and less and less.

00:14:14.600 --> 00:14:15.920
Yeah, more time goes.

00:14:15.920 --> 00:14:18.920
And I think a lot more companies
need to start doing the same.

00:14:18.960 --> 00:14:21.400
When you say we will then design

00:14:21.400 --> 00:14:24.920
for the next level,
the order of magnitude for the next level,

00:14:25.320 --> 00:14:29.520
how far in the horizon do you look at what
the GitHub actions example that you had?

00:14:29.520 --> 00:14:30.720
You said, okay, five years,

00:14:30.720 --> 00:14:33.840
but is that like an arbitrary thing
or how depends on the curve, right.

00:14:33.840 --> 00:14:36.840
It depends on the GitHub like GitHub
actions has been growing massively over

00:14:36.880 --> 00:14:41.600
since we, we we brought it up,
at the beginning, and we're introducing

00:14:41.600 --> 00:14:44.600
more and more features
that are increasing the adoption.

00:14:45.200 --> 00:14:47.760
So the curve we can see how it looks like.

00:14:47.760 --> 00:14:49.600
Yeah. But it might change at any point
in time. Right.

00:14:49.600 --> 00:14:53.400
It can be disrupted close to zero
or it can continue growing exponentially.

00:14:53.880 --> 00:14:57.480
So often the hockey stick
that a lot of tech startups

00:14:57.480 --> 00:15:01.320
talk about, like exponential growth,
it can appear and you can start seeing it.

00:15:01.320 --> 00:15:02.680
And when you start seeing it,

00:15:02.680 --> 00:15:06.160
you know, see how the solution
like actions, companies that migrate to it

00:15:06.160 --> 00:15:07.680
are not going to flip flop. No.

00:15:07.680 --> 00:15:10.680
And very short periods of time be there
so you can have some confidence that,

00:15:10.680 --> 00:15:15.120
okay, this trend is going to last
for a year or two or 3 or 4, right?

00:15:15.120 --> 00:15:17.400
Nothing lasts forever, obviously.

00:15:17.400 --> 00:15:19.720
But you can start thinking
in that direction.

00:15:19.720 --> 00:15:22.000
And then based on that,
you can make investment decisions.

00:15:22.000 --> 00:15:22.960
Gotcha.

00:15:22.960 --> 00:15:26.320
I've had many discussions also in the past
when we were talking about our software

00:15:26.320 --> 00:15:29.640
architecture
in something that can be very specific

00:15:29.640 --> 00:15:32.880
and also purposeful, built versus
setting it up in a generic way.

00:15:33.280 --> 00:15:35.880
But then it should be towards the future,
because we have ambitions

00:15:35.880 --> 00:15:39.120
and hopes that we have
X amount of solutions that are similar.

00:15:39.120 --> 00:15:42.280
So we make a generic right now,
what is your perspective on

00:15:42.280 --> 00:15:45.280
making something specific versus
generic in the first place?

00:15:46.080 --> 00:15:48.240
I like solving specific problems.

00:15:48.240 --> 00:15:52.360
I don't like solving generic problems
because how can you make tradeoffs then?

00:15:53.040 --> 00:15:53.320
Right.

00:15:53.320 --> 00:15:55.600
Well, in software
there's always trade offs.

00:15:55.600 --> 00:15:58.160
So how do you choose these trade offs?

00:15:58.160 --> 00:16:00.240
You can build something
super generic. Sure.

00:16:00.240 --> 00:16:04.160
That basically is why building a framework
that can be a toolbox

00:16:04.160 --> 00:16:07.400
and give you all sorts of solutions
to all sorts

00:16:07.400 --> 00:16:10.400
of hypothetical problems
that you might or might not have.

00:16:10.480 --> 00:16:13.640
That's not the Northstar
I follow when I design software at GitHub.

00:16:13.960 --> 00:16:15.280
With my team, we

00:16:16.560 --> 00:16:17.040
are very

00:16:17.040 --> 00:16:20.040
specific about not overengineering things,

00:16:20.280 --> 00:16:22.920
and we solve for the problems
we have today.

00:16:22.920 --> 00:16:26.440
So for example, we never introduce caching
until we really need it,

00:16:27.680 --> 00:16:31.080
and we never over optimize
until we start seeing problems.

00:16:31.440 --> 00:16:34.880
Sometimes, sometimes we we know
that we're going to hit this problem

00:16:34.880 --> 00:16:36.480
a few months
and we're not going to get the investment.

00:16:36.480 --> 00:16:39.160
So we solve it right now
because it makes sense.

00:16:39.160 --> 00:16:41.320
But we don't over engineer solutions.

00:16:41.320 --> 00:16:43.000
We don't start jumping into things.

00:16:43.000 --> 00:16:46.440
For example, we don't necessarily jump
to NoSQL at the beginning.

00:16:46.480 --> 00:16:48.960
We just start regular
relational databases.

00:16:48.960 --> 00:16:52.600
You know, we build regular tables
if we need if we need actually relational,

00:16:52.880 --> 00:16:54.000
content.

00:16:54.000 --> 00:16:57.000
Sometimes relational databases
can always get so far.

00:16:57.480 --> 00:17:01.320
So we start needing to three
think our solutions here.

00:17:01.640 --> 00:17:03.440
And we need to start going towards
non-relational.

00:17:03.440 --> 00:17:06.080
Even if we need
the relational consistency,

00:17:06.080 --> 00:17:09.880
or the consistency of relational
databases, we need to start thinking about

00:17:09.880 --> 00:17:12.120
how can we move that to the application
layer

00:17:12.120 --> 00:17:16.680
and start using other solutions
that persist data that can help us scale?

00:17:16.840 --> 00:17:20.520
Yeah, there are some efforts, for example,
where we start using cosmos or

00:17:21.320 --> 00:17:22.760
cosmos DB and things like that,

00:17:24.280 --> 00:17:24.960
but we

00:17:24.960 --> 00:17:27.960
never start exploring these
until we hit really massive problems

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:31.000
with our database clusters
like our primaries cannot keep up.

00:17:31.000 --> 00:17:32.720
The IO is pretty high.

00:17:32.720 --> 00:17:35.160
Right? Operations are substantially big.

00:17:35.160 --> 00:17:38.160
We cannot scale these anymore.

00:17:38.400 --> 00:17:40.000
Sharding is not an option.

00:17:40.000 --> 00:17:42.720
Application
layer sharding is not an option as well.

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:45.720
When it becomes not an option,
sometimes it is.

00:17:45.800 --> 00:17:48.960
So we started thinking about
how can we design this

00:17:48.960 --> 00:17:53.040
and how can we migrate the data and
migrations as much as they are painful,

00:17:53.400 --> 00:17:54.520
they are part of the job.

00:17:54.520 --> 00:17:55.600
Yeah, interesting.

00:17:55.600 --> 00:17:57.360
And we do go through them.

00:17:57.360 --> 00:17:59.520
I'm wondering
when we talk about system design

00:17:59.520 --> 00:18:01.720
and then specifically
practical experience,

00:18:01.720 --> 00:18:04.520
what your perspective is on
someone to really get

00:18:04.520 --> 00:18:07.520
a better understanding of the theory,
but also be able to put it in practice.

00:18:07.520 --> 00:18:10.440
You like you already mentioned that,
for example, like GitHub,

00:18:10.440 --> 00:18:13.440
you need to have a certain
level of experience under your belt

00:18:13.440 --> 00:18:16.960
and then you can work at GitHub
where we have orders of magnitude

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:19.200
with regards to scale.
This is a vicious cycle, right?

00:18:19.200 --> 00:18:21.880
So do you. How do you
how do you build experience

00:18:21.880 --> 00:18:23.640
that is required
by this type of companies.

00:18:23.640 --> 00:18:27.320
But there's no other type of company
that's going to give you that experience.

00:18:27.800 --> 00:18:31.840
Unfortunately there's that's that's
the reason why there is a proliferation

00:18:31.840 --> 00:18:35.120
of a lot of system
design courses and things online.

00:18:35.760 --> 00:18:37.600
And quite honestly,

00:18:37.600 --> 00:18:40.800
passing that into passing the interview
threshold for companies like GitHub.

00:18:42.080 --> 00:18:42.600
That is

00:18:42.600 --> 00:18:46.200
achievable by learning
theoretically these concepts, okay,

00:18:46.200 --> 00:18:49.200
and not necessarily
having hands on experience with them.

00:18:49.400 --> 00:18:52.160
The bar feels high,

00:18:52.160 --> 00:18:55.160
but right now it's been sort of

00:18:56.040 --> 00:18:58.680
exploited to the point
where a lot of the tricks

00:18:58.680 --> 00:19:02.000
and tips and whatnot are already available
very much online.

00:19:02.320 --> 00:19:05.320
Like, I know, interview you guys, for
example, they have this YouTube channel.

00:19:05.320 --> 00:19:08.320
They have pretty much covered
a lot of the topics that you might get

00:19:08.320 --> 00:19:10.080
asked in a system design interview.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:10.400
Okay.

00:19:10.400 --> 00:19:12.320
And they go deep
like they explain what Kafka

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:13.800
is, what event streaming, as they explain.

00:19:13.800 --> 00:19:15.880
Right, is the internals, how it works,
when do you use it?

00:19:15.880 --> 00:19:16.960
When not to use it?

00:19:16.960 --> 00:19:19.960
They explain the differences between
relational and relational databases.

00:19:19.960 --> 00:19:22.120
They explain caching, how to implement

00:19:22.120 --> 00:19:25.880
caching in specific scenarios,
and they give practical, hands on problems

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:27.160
where you can actually learn

00:19:27.160 --> 00:19:30.160
how to effectively utilize
all of these different components

00:19:30.360 --> 00:19:33.360
to come up
with a relatively scalable solution.

00:19:33.560 --> 00:19:36.480
And they give you tips
also on how to approach

00:19:36.480 --> 00:19:39.480
the discussion of scale
within a system design interview.

00:19:40.040 --> 00:19:41.640
And I think
they do a pretty good job at it.

00:19:41.640 --> 00:19:44.520
And I think that's enough,
because a lot of people who go to big

00:19:44.520 --> 00:19:47.560
Tech will go to Google, and they don't
necessarily have that hands on experience

00:19:47.560 --> 00:19:51.040
beforehand, even though the interview
is looking for that hands on experience.

00:19:51.480 --> 00:19:55.040
But, you know, with these,
because we hack the people.

00:19:55.040 --> 00:19:56.960
Hack the game. Yeah.

00:19:56.960 --> 00:19:59.800
So the signal is given to the interviewer.

00:19:59.800 --> 00:20:03.000
And then people when they have these,
this foundation, they can actually

00:20:03.240 --> 00:20:06.440
do a relatively
okay job on, when they join

00:20:06.800 --> 00:20:08.640
and they've got obviously
not going to be a right.

00:20:08.640 --> 00:20:10.400
So you're never going to join a team
and start

00:20:10.400 --> 00:20:12.240
building greenfield stuff from day zero.

00:20:12.240 --> 00:20:15.920
It rarely happens where you're going to
join teams that are already established.

00:20:15.920 --> 00:20:17.040
So there's going to be people

00:20:17.040 --> 00:20:20.040
with experience more senior
than you are going to give you guidance

00:20:20.040 --> 00:20:22.880
and review your solutions, your designs,
so on and so forth.

00:20:22.880 --> 00:20:25.200
And that's why people make it right.

00:20:25.200 --> 00:20:28.640
But if you are interviewed for experience
and you join a greenfield project,

00:20:29.240 --> 00:20:31.680
you know, you going to own your mistakes.

00:20:31.680 --> 00:20:33.720
And people do make mistakes often.

00:20:33.720 --> 00:20:34.000
Yeah.

00:20:34.000 --> 00:20:36.360
When it comes to these solutions,
not everything big tech

00:20:36.360 --> 00:20:40.640
ships is a great feature or operates
well in the world.

00:20:40.640 --> 00:20:45.720
And often we discover that, oh, this was a
mistake and it was actually very costly.

00:20:45.720 --> 00:20:48.720
And some companies fail
because of this researcher.

00:20:48.720 --> 00:20:49.160
Right?

00:20:49.160 --> 00:20:52.080
Any failures or mistakes
that are top of mind for you

00:20:52.080 --> 00:20:53.560
that you can share
for other people to learn?

00:20:54.520 --> 00:20:54.920
Tricky.

00:20:54.920 --> 00:20:57.920
No, I want to keep my job so

00:20:58.360 --> 00:20:59.040
that's fair.

00:20:59.040 --> 00:21:00.840
That's fair for me.

00:21:00.840 --> 00:21:02.840
Looking at what we do nowadays,

00:21:02.840 --> 00:21:06.080
I feel with AI tools and we're
more productive in actually writing code.

00:21:06.600 --> 00:21:09.760
And I feel like when you amplify
the amount of code that you write,

00:21:10.080 --> 00:21:13.600
you might also write yourself
into a bottleneck, basically.

00:21:13.600 --> 00:21:17.680
So I feel like software architecture and
specifically system design from the start,

00:21:17.680 --> 00:21:22.000
having a better understanding,
especially I feel like like I do now,

00:21:22.560 --> 00:21:25.800
I need to grow there and I feel like it's
going to be a more important skill

00:21:25.800 --> 00:21:29.600
to indeed figure out what are we designing
for, what makes sense?

00:21:29.600 --> 00:21:32.760
What would be the next order of magnitude
after, and actually

00:21:33.080 --> 00:21:35.800
make sure to convince people
and have a certain level of buy in.

00:21:35.800 --> 00:21:37.360
We're always working in teams.

00:21:37.360 --> 00:21:40.440
Also, from a business standpoint,
how would I, for example, say

00:21:40.680 --> 00:21:42.720
we're going to design for this
until we reach scale

00:21:42.720 --> 00:21:45.840
and then we're going to need to revamp,
and this is what that's going to entail

00:21:46.080 --> 00:21:48.600
for people that don't understand
how to build software,

00:21:48.600 --> 00:21:50.280
understand the business constraints.

00:21:50.280 --> 00:21:53.400
So you're never going to be able
to convince the business folks

00:21:53.400 --> 00:21:54.280
about of something.

00:21:54.280 --> 00:21:57.280
Technically,
they're just it's too abstract.

00:21:57.640 --> 00:22:02.400
Even if it feels too intuitive
for us, for them, it's magic.

00:22:02.520 --> 00:22:03.360
Yeah.

00:22:03.360 --> 00:22:05.840
And even if they try to understand

00:22:05.840 --> 00:22:09.120
how things work, they're never going
to comprehend the magnitude of problems.

00:22:09.120 --> 00:22:12.400
And they're never going to 
have this intuition about how software is

00:22:12.400 --> 00:22:16.800
and how not hard,
you know, how how involved it is.

00:22:17.720 --> 00:22:20.880
So it's our job
to get closer to their side.

00:22:21.120 --> 00:22:23.280
Some people say, no,
we want the business to understand.

00:22:23.280 --> 00:22:24.360
They're not gonna.

00:22:24.360 --> 00:22:25.880
It's trickier.

00:22:25.880 --> 00:22:29.800
It's easier for you to acquire, assimilate
some of the not everything,

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:33.800
some of the business constraints
and discuss them from that perspective.

00:22:34.040 --> 00:22:38.160
And that's why a lot of engineers
who grow are very good at understanding

00:22:38.160 --> 00:22:39.480
the business constraints

00:22:39.480 --> 00:22:42.480
and then discussing the technical
solutions within those constraints.

00:22:42.600 --> 00:22:47.480
So for example, I used to work in the
container terminal industry for a while.

00:22:47.480 --> 00:22:50.440
We built a huge container terminal.

00:22:50.440 --> 00:22:52.320
We furnished everything
from the fiber optics

00:22:52.320 --> 00:22:54.360
layer
all the way to the three tier data center.

00:22:54.360 --> 00:22:57.040
With all of the enterprise applications
running and running on it,

00:22:57.040 --> 00:22:59.080
and the operating system
for the container,

00:22:59.080 --> 00:23:02.120
and it's one of the most
massive operations you can ever imagine.

00:23:03.120 --> 00:23:05.720
One of the most hazardous as well.

00:23:05.720 --> 00:23:10.320
So the folks, they're there
as far away from tech as possible.

00:23:10.560 --> 00:23:12.560
You're not never going
to find the terminal operator

00:23:12.560 --> 00:23:13.960
who's going to understand what it means

00:23:13.960 --> 00:23:17.520
that your database is running hot
and you cannot support their case.

00:23:17.520 --> 00:23:20.520
All they care about is, I have a ship
here, was docked.

00:23:21.200 --> 00:23:23.520
There's like, 10,000 containers on it.

00:23:23.520 --> 00:23:25.360
I need to empty them into my yard.

00:23:25.360 --> 00:23:28.280
I have a very fixed window of time,
and if I don't do it

00:23:28.280 --> 00:23:31.280
in that window of time,
this is going to cost me money.

00:23:32.400 --> 00:23:34.440
We were operating at 12% capacity.

00:23:34.440 --> 00:23:38.680
That terminal was generating
about $50,000 per hour.

00:23:40.160 --> 00:23:42.040
So 12%.

00:23:42.040 --> 00:23:44.680
So you can imagine if we get delayed

00:23:44.680 --> 00:23:48.240
every minute of delay is actually money
that's disappearing.

00:23:49.160 --> 00:23:53.640
So this is how you need to discuss
problems and investments and solutions.

00:23:54.040 --> 00:23:57.240
You need to understand what the impact
is, what the impact is on the operators.

00:23:57.240 --> 00:23:59.400
And sometimes it's life threatening
impact, right.

00:23:59.400 --> 00:24:02.520
If your software fails when a STS

00:24:03.120 --> 00:24:05.920
when the crane is offloading a container,

00:24:05.920 --> 00:24:09.120
this can be a very life
threatening situation.

00:24:09.280 --> 00:24:12.360
You cannot really have your software
malfunction at this point.

00:24:12.360 --> 00:24:16.320
So sloppiness of the software
sub millisecond latencies,

00:24:16.320 --> 00:24:18.160
all of these things matter, right?

00:24:18.160 --> 00:24:21.280
So you need to approach the discussions
with the stakeholders. Yes.

00:24:21.360 --> 00:24:22.640
By understanding them first.

00:24:22.640 --> 00:24:24.480
So what I've done, for
example, in the industry is

00:24:25.720 --> 00:24:28.360
I've literally sat on a truck

00:24:28.360 --> 00:24:29.200
and went there

00:24:29.200 --> 00:24:31.680
and I sat with every single individual
who works in that terminal

00:24:31.680 --> 00:24:34.200
to really understand
what they go through on a daily basis.

00:24:34.200 --> 00:24:38.040
So a truck driver who's driving a truck
for six, seven, eight hours

00:24:38.040 --> 00:24:42.360
and it was in Saudi Arabia in scorching
heat, it's not going to understand

00:24:42.360 --> 00:24:45.360
like the button that they clicked
is just it's so

